1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:02,197
You're falling into a black hole.

2
00:00:02,217 --> 00:00:03,167
And the first thing it

3
00:00:03,167 --> 00:00:04,128
does to you...

4
00:00:04,148 --> 00:00:05,410
is stretch you like spaghetti.

5
00:00:06,410 --> 00:00:06,935
But first,

6
00:00:06,935 --> 00:00:07,918
a myth.

7
00:00:07,938 --> 00:00:09,091
Black holes don't suck things

8
00:00:09,091 --> 00:00:10,498
in like a vacuum cleaner.

9
00:00:10,518 --> 00:00:11,466
If the Sun were swapped

10
00:00:11,466 --> 00:00:12,488
for a black hole with

11
00:00:12,488 --> 00:00:13,101
the same mass,

12
00:00:13,101 --> 00:00:14,420
Earth would keep orbiting just

13
00:00:14,420 --> 00:00:15,123
as before.

14
00:00:15,143 --> 00:00:16,037
It would just get very

15
00:00:16,037 --> 00:00:17,303
dark, and very cold.

16
00:00:20,304 --> 00:00:21,440
A black hole is matter

17
00:00:21,440 --> 00:00:22,824
squeezed so tightly that its

18
00:00:22,824 --> 00:00:24,220
gravity traps everything.

19
00:00:24,240 --> 00:00:25,167
If you could squeeze the

20
00:00:25,167 --> 00:00:26,220
Sun into one,

21
00:00:26,240 --> 00:00:27,662
it would be only about

22
00:00:27,662 --> 00:00:29,398
three kilometres in radius.

23
00:00:30,399 --> 00:00:31,570
Many black holes are born

24
00:00:31,570 --> 00:00:32,859
when a giant star,

25
00:00:32,879 --> 00:00:34,189
much heavier than our Sun,

26
00:00:34,209 --> 00:00:35,303
runs out of fuel.

27
00:00:35,323 --> 00:00:36,616
Its core collapses under its

28
00:00:36,616 --> 00:00:37,563
own weight,

29
00:00:37,583 --> 00:00:37,743
and keeps collapsing,

30
00:00:37,763 --> 00:00:41,247
into a point of incredible density.

31
00:00:41,267 --> 00:00:42,175
And Earth?

32
00:00:42,195 --> 00:00:43,993
About nine millimetres.

33
00:00:44,013 --> 00:00:45,670
Smaller than a marble.

34
00:00:45,690 --> 00:00:46,402
Same mass,

35
00:00:46,402 --> 00:00:47,661
unbelievably small.

36
00:00:49,011 --> 00:00:50,082
Its edge is called the

37
00:00:50,082 --> 00:00:51,132
event horizon.

38
00:00:51,152 --> 00:00:52,392
It's not a wall or

39
00:00:52,392 --> 00:00:53,268
a surface.

40
00:00:53,288 --> 00:00:54,780
It's a point of no return.

41
00:00:54,800 --> 00:00:55,720
Once you're inside it,

42
00:00:55,740 --> 00:00:57,030
nothing can get back out.

43
00:00:57,050 --> 00:00:58,349
Not even light.

44
00:00:58,369 --> 00:00:59,257
That's why it's black.

45
00:01:00,607 --> 00:01:02,384
Now, the fall.

46
00:01:02,404 --> 00:01:03,645
Gravity pulls much harder on

47
00:01:03,645 --> 00:01:04,364
your feet,

48
00:01:04,384 --> 00:01:05,354
which are closer,

49
00:01:05,374 --> 00:01:06,236
than on your head.

50
00:01:06,256 --> 00:01:07,334
That difference stretches you

51
00:01:07,334 --> 00:01:08,147
long and thin.

52
00:01:08,167 --> 00:01:11,432
Scientists really call it spaghettification.

53
00:01:11,452 --> 00:01:12,925
Here's the twist.

54
00:01:12,945 --> 00:01:13,854
The size of the black

55
00:01:13,854 --> 00:01:15,216
hole changes everything.

56
00:01:15,236 --> 00:01:16,186
Near a small one,

57
00:01:16,206 --> 00:01:17,517
about ten times the Sun's

58
00:01:17,517 --> 00:01:19,346
mass, you'd be stretched apart

59
00:01:19,346 --> 00:01:20,584
well before you even reached

60
00:01:20,584 --> 00:01:20,943
the edge.

61
00:01:21,943 --> 00:01:23,256
But near a supermassive black

62
00:01:23,256 --> 00:01:24,753
hole, the stretching at the

63
00:01:24,753 --> 00:01:25,976
edge is so gentle that

64
00:01:25,976 --> 00:01:27,157
you could cross it without

65
00:01:27,157 --> 00:01:28,972
feeling anything special at first.

66
00:01:28,992 --> 00:01:29,478
Deeper in,

67
00:01:29,478 --> 00:01:30,451
though, the stretching would

68
00:01:30,451 --> 00:01:31,918
still win.

69
00:01:31,938 --> 00:01:33,390
And we've actually seen them.

70
00:01:33,410 --> 00:01:35,150
In 2019,

71
00:01:35,170 --> 00:01:36,585
the Event Horizon Telescope

72
00:01:36,585 --> 00:01:37,770
showed the first image of

73
00:01:37,770 --> 00:01:38,910
a black hole,

74
00:01:38,930 --> 00:01:40,960
called M87 star.

75
00:01:40,980 --> 00:01:42,763
It's about 55 million

76
00:01:42,763 --> 00:01:44,050
light-years away,

77
00:01:44,070 --> 00:01:45,961
and about 6.5 billion times

78
00:01:45,961 --> 00:01:47,486
the mass of the Sun.

79
00:01:47,506 --> 00:01:48,565
The light in that photo

80
00:01:48,565 --> 00:01:49,996
left it about 55 million

81
00:01:49,996 --> 00:01:50,856
years ago,

82
00:01:50,876 --> 00:01:51,866
long before there were any

83
00:01:51,866 --> 00:01:52,704
humans to see it.

84
00:01:54,054 --> 00:01:55,003
How do you photograph

85
00:01:55,003 --> 00:01:56,046
something that's black?

86
00:01:56,066 --> 00:01:57,490
Astronomers linked radio

87
00:01:57,490 --> 00:01:58,849
telescopes all around the

88
00:01:58,849 --> 00:02:00,624
world into one giant virtual

89
00:02:00,624 --> 00:02:02,137
telescope, as big as the Earth.

90
00:02:03,137 --> 00:02:05,365
In 2022 we saw Sagittarius

91
00:02:05,365 --> 00:02:06,257
A star,

92
00:02:06,277 --> 00:02:07,173
the black hole at the

93
00:02:07,173 --> 00:02:09,115
centre of our own Milky Way.

94
00:02:09,135 --> 00:02:11,505
About 27,000 light-years away,

95
00:02:11,525 --> 00:02:12,930
about 4 million times the

96
00:02:12,930 --> 00:02:14,045
Sun's mass.

97
00:02:14,065 --> 00:02:15,230
And its dark centre would

98
00:02:15,230 --> 00:02:17,829
fit inside the orbit of Mercury.

99
00:02:17,849 --> 00:02:19,029
Fun twist:

100
00:02:19,049 --> 00:02:20,527
M87 star is about a

101
00:02:20,527 --> 00:02:21,813
thousand times bigger than our

102
00:02:21,813 --> 00:02:23,323
galaxy's black hole.

103
00:02:23,343 --> 00:02:24,829
But it's so much farther

104
00:02:24,829 --> 00:02:25,533
away that,

105
00:02:25,553 --> 00:02:25,995
in the sky,

106
00:02:25,995 --> 00:02:28,029
both look about the same size.

107
00:02:28,049 --> 00:02:29,299
Now imagine a friend watching

108
00:02:29,299 --> 00:02:30,564
you fall from far away.

109
00:02:30,584 --> 00:02:30,997
To them,

110
00:02:30,997 --> 00:02:32,474
you'd seem to slow down,

111
00:02:32,494 --> 00:02:33,554
turn redder and dimmer,

112
00:02:33,574 --> 00:02:34,584
and fade away,

113
00:02:34,604 --> 00:02:36,947
as if frozen near the edge.

114
00:02:36,967 --> 00:02:37,889
That's because gravity

115
00:02:37,889 --> 00:02:39,247
stretches time and light.

116
00:02:39,267 --> 00:02:40,225
From your own point of

117
00:02:40,225 --> 00:02:41,693
view, nothing freezes at all.

118
00:02:41,713 --> 00:02:42,706
You just keep falling.

119
00:02:44,055 --> 00:02:45,183
Should we worry?

120
00:02:45,203 --> 00:02:46,809
The nearest black hole we

121
00:02:46,809 --> 00:02:47,653
know of,

122
00:02:47,673 --> 00:02:48,383
Gaia BH1,

123
00:02:48,403 --> 00:02:52,543
is about 1,560 light-years away.

124
00:02:52,563 --> 00:02:53,626
Scientists think there are

125
00:02:53,626 --> 00:02:54,762
probably closer ones we

126
00:02:54,762 --> 00:02:55,806
haven't found yet.

127
00:02:55,826 --> 00:02:56,626
But close,

128
00:02:56,646 --> 00:02:57,098
in space,

129
00:02:57,098 --> 00:02:57,796
is still very,

130
00:02:57,816 --> 00:02:58,475
very far.

131
00:02:59,475 --> 00:03:00,822
Sometimes black holes crash

132
00:03:00,822 --> 00:03:01,890
into each other.

133
00:03:01,910 --> 00:03:03,640
On the 14th of September,

134
00:03:03,660 --> 00:03:05,787
2015, LIGO detected

135
00:03:05,787 --> 00:03:07,034
gravitational waves for the

136
00:03:07,034 --> 00:03:07,840
first time,

137
00:03:07,860 --> 00:03:09,150
from two black holes,

138
00:03:09,170 --> 00:03:11,007
about 36 and 29 times

139
00:03:11,007 --> 00:03:12,097
the Sun's mass, merging.

140
00:03:13,447 --> 00:03:14,691
About three Suns' worth of

141
00:03:14,691 --> 00:03:16,016
mass turned into ripples in

142
00:03:16,016 --> 00:03:16,930
space itself.

143
00:03:16,950 --> 00:03:18,020
For a split second,

144
00:03:18,040 --> 00:03:19,088
that gave off more power

145
00:03:19,088 --> 00:03:20,029
than all the stars in

146
00:03:20,029 --> 00:03:21,040
the visible universe,

147
00:03:21,060 --> 00:03:22,589
put together.

148
00:03:22,609 --> 00:03:23,915
So, could you survive falling

149
00:03:23,915 --> 00:03:25,169
into a black hole?

150
00:03:25,189 --> 00:03:25,859
Pick a giant one,

151
00:03:25,879 --> 00:03:26,859
and you'd at least enjoy

152
00:03:26,859 --> 00:03:27,993
the view for a while.

153
00:03:28,013 --> 00:03:28,927
A small one?

154
00:03:28,947 --> 00:03:30,535
Not a great idea.

155
00:03:30,555 --> 00:03:31,022
Either way,

156
00:03:31,022 --> 00:03:32,011
the black hole wins in

157
00:03:32,011 --> 00:03:32,591
the end.

158
00:03:32,611 --> 00:03:33,171
Next time,

159
00:03:33,171 --> 00:03:34,342
we go the other way.

160
00:03:34,362 --> 00:03:35,312
Down.

161
00:03:35,332 --> 00:03:37,342
About eleven kilometres down,

162
00:03:37,362 --> 00:03:38,174
to the very bottom of

163
00:03:38,174 --> 00:03:38,732
the ocean,

164
00:03:38,752 --> 00:03:40,164
where the water itself becomes

165
00:03:40,164 --> 00:03:40,755
the danger.

166
00:03:42,154 --> 00:03:43,082
Would you jump into a

167
00:03:43,082 --> 00:03:43,602
giant one,

168
00:03:43,602 --> 00:03:44,485
just to see?

169
00:03:44,505 --> 00:03:45,812
Tell me in the comments.

170
00:03:45,832 --> 00:03:46,792
Next time,

171
00:03:46,812 --> 00:03:47,955
we stop falling up and

172
00:03:47,955 --> 00:03:49,126
start sinking down.

173
00:03:49,146 --> 00:03:50,854
Subscribe, and bring a very

174
00:03:50,854 --> 00:03:51,521
strong submarine.
